      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Deep_River,_Washington",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Finnish immigrants arrived in the 1870s, fleeing famine and Russification, and built Deep River into a hub of Finnish-American culture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        7,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Deep_River,_Washington",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The community sits 4.5 miles upstream from the Columbia River estuary, surrounded by low-lying riverine terrain and dense forests.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Deep_River,_Washington",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1901, a railroad built by the Deep River Logging Company linked the town to the Columbia River, boosting timber extraction.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Deep_River,_Washington",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Salmon fishing peaked in the late 19th century, with canneries employing many residents before declining after 1947.",
